# A fold over the tree

A pass over a loom's tree changes a few kinds of node and leaves the rest as it found them. A pass that expands a heading's label, say, changes headings, and every weft and token of the loom passes through it untouched. Written by hand, each such pass would repeat one long walk over the whole tree to reach the few nodes it changes.

The `rewritten` function is that walk, written once. A pass hands it a tree and one case for each kind of node the pass changes. The function walks the tree, calls a case wherever a node of its kind stands, and rebuilds every other node around the results. The chapter "Small passes over the tree" describes the passes that use it.

## A node carries a tag

A tree holds three sorts of value. A node is an object with a `type` tag, as every node that the `loomNode` helper builds is. An array holds values in order, as a document holds its sections. Everything else is a plain value: a string, a number, or an object without a tag, such as a position or a health. The fold takes a plain value whole and never looks inside it.

=>

export type Tagged = { readonly type: string }

type Node = Tagged & Readonly<Record<string, unknown>>

const isNode = (value: unknown): value is Node =>
  typeof value === 'object' && value !== null && 'type' in value && typeof value.type === 'string'

## A pass names its edits

A pass describes its work as edits. An edit pairs a tag with the node the pass writes wherever a node of that tag stands. A pass that expands labels has one edit: under the `LoomHeading` tag, a heading whose braces hold parameters only.

A case carries out one edit. It is a function that takes the node a tree holds under the edit's tag and returns the node the pass writes. The `NodesOf` type gathers every kind of node a tree can hold, at any depth. The `Cases` type uses it to hand each case the exact node its tag names in that tree, so a case written for a heading cannot be filed under the tag of a section.

=>

export type NodesOf<Tree> =
  Tree extends ReadonlyArray<infer Item>
    ? NodesOf<Item>
    : Tree extends Tagged
      ? Tree | { readonly [Key in keyof Tree]-?: NodesOf<Tree[Key]> }[keyof Tree]
      : never

export type Cases<Tree, Edits> = {
  readonly [Tag in keyof Edits]: (
    node: Extract<NodesOf<Tree>, { readonly type: Tag }>,
  ) => Edits[Tag]
}

## The tree a pass writes

The `Rewritten` type is the type of a tree after a pass. A node whose tag has an edit becomes the edit's node. Every other node keeps its fields, each rewritten in turn. A plain value stays as it is.

The compiler works this type out from the tree and the edits. A pass states the tree it writes as its return type, and the pass fails to compile when its edits write any other tree.

=>

export type Rewritten<Tree, Edits> =
  Tree extends ReadonlyArray<infer Item>
    ? ReadonlyArray<Rewritten<Item, Edits>>
    : Tree extends { readonly type: infer Tag }
      ? Tag extends keyof Edits
        ? Edits[Tag]
        : { readonly [Key in keyof Tree]: Rewritten<Tree[Key], Edits> }
      : Tree

## The walk

The `rewritten` function walks a tree from its root down. It is the one place where the types guard a pass: the function takes the pass's typed cases and returns the typed tree. Inside it, the `walked` function works on values it knows nothing about, and that is what lets one walk serve every tree.

=>

type Case = (node: Node) => unknown

type AnyCases = Readonly<Record<string, Case>>

export const rewritten = <Tree, Edits>(
  tree: Tree,
  cases: Cases<Tree, Edits>,
): Rewritten<Tree, Edits> => walked(tree, cases as AnyCases) as Rewritten<Tree, Edits>

~

The `walked` function meets one of four things:

- **An array.** It walks each item.
- **A node with a case.** It calls the case and takes what the case returns as it stands. The walk does not enter the returned node, so the pass alone decides what its edit holds.
- **A node without a case.** It walks each of the node's fields.
- **A plain value.** It returns the value.

A case is found by its own key alone. The `Record.get` function reads only the keys a pass wrote, so a node tagged `constructor` never meets the method every object inherits under that name.

=>

const walked = (value: unknown, cases: AnyCases): unknown =>
  Match.value(value).pipe(
    Match.when(Array.isArray, (items) => walkedItems(items, cases)),
    Match.when(isNode, (node) =>
      Option.match(Record.get(cases, node.type), {
        onSome: (edit) => edit(node),
        onNone: () => walkedFields(node, cases),
      }),
    ),
    Match.orElse((plain) => plain),
  )

~

A walk copies nothing it leaves unchanged. The `walkedItems` function returns the array it was given when every item came back as the same object, and the `walkedFields` function does the same for a node. The part of a tree that a pass leaves alone stays the very objects the pass received. A row of passes over a large loom then allocates only what the passes write.

=>

const walkedItems = (items: ReadonlyArray<unknown>, cases: AnyCases): ReadonlyArray<unknown> => {
  const next = Array.map(items, (item) => walked(item, cases))
  return Array.every(next, (item, at) => item === items[at]) ? items : next
}

const walkedFields = (node: Node, cases: AnyCases): Node => {
  const next = Record.map(node, (field) => walked(field, cases))
  return Record.every(next, (field, key) => field === node[key])
    ? node
    : { ...next, type: node.type }
}

## What this module draws on

The walk dispatches with Effect's `Match`, finds a case through `Option` and `Record`, and maps arrays with `Array`.

=>

import { Array, Match, Option, Record } from 'effect'

